From: board fix
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: yt921x: program the global port-enable mask

On the Xiaomi AX3000E the switch came up with the CPU SERDES trained at
2.5Gbps and all four internal PHYs enumerating correctly, yet no user port
ever reported carrier and every FDB operation timed out ("FDB probably
stuck", -110).

The cause is an undocumented global port-enable mask at 0x8002c. A chip reset
leaves it at 0xff, which disables most of the fabric. This is not in any
datasheet -- it was found during the QSDK-driver work on this board by dumping
all 256 registers of the 0x80000 block and diffing against a capture taken
from a known-good vendor boot, where it reads 0x1fff.

--- a/drivers/net/dsa/yt921x.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/yt921x.c
@@ -188,6 +188,10 @@
 	return priv->reg_ops->read(priv->reg_ctx, reg, valp);
 }
 
+/* Undocumented: global port-enable mask (see yt921x_setup()). */
+#define YT921X_PORT_EN			0x8002c
+#define YT921X_PORT_EN_ALL		0x1fff
+
 static int yt921x_reg_write(struct yt921x_priv *priv, u32 reg, u32 val)
 {
 	WARN_ON(!mutex_is_locked(&priv->reg_lock));
@@ -2803,6 +2807,18 @@
 
 	mutex_lock(&priv->reg_lock);
 	res = yt921x_chip_reset(priv);
+	if (!res) {
+		/* Undocumented global port-enable mask. A chip reset leaves it
+		 * at 0xff, which keeps most ports -- including the CPU uplink
+		 * -- disabled in the fabric, so user ports never show carrier
+		 * and FDB operations time out. 0x1fff is the value read back
+		 * from a known-good vendor boot on the Xiaomi AX3000E
+		 * (YT9215S). The register is not described in any datasheet;
+		 * it was found by diffing all 256 registers of the 0x80000
+		 * block against a working stock capture.
+		 */
+		res = yt921x_reg_write(priv, YT921X_PORT_EN, YT921X_PORT_EN_ALL);
+	}
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->reg_lock);
 
 	if (res)
